## Proof

**Answer: Yes, $g$ is locally Lipschitz in the stated sense.**

Let $K \subset \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^{n'} \times [0,1]$ be a nonempty compact set. We must produce $M > 0$ such that for all $(x_1, y_1, t), (x_2, y_2, t) \in K$,
$$
\|g(x_1, y_1, t) - g(x_2, y_2, t)\| \le M\bigl(\|x_1 - x_2\| + \|y_1 - y_2\|\bigr).
$$

**Step 1: Construct a compact set containing all relevant line segments.**

Define
$$
S = \Bigl\{\bigl((x_1, y_1, t),\, (x_2, y_2, t),\, s\bigr) \in K \times K \times [0,1] : (x_1, y_1, t), (x_2, y_2, t) \in K,\; s \in [0,1]\Bigr\}.
$$
The constraint "the two $K$-points share the same $t$" is a closed condition (it is the zero set of the continuous map $((p_1, p_2, s) \mapsto t_1 - t_2)$). Hence $S$ is a closed subset of the compact set $K \times K \times [0,1]$, and is therefore compact.

Define the continuous map $\Phi : S \to \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^{n'} \times [0,1]$ by
$$
\Phi\bigl((x_1, y_1, t),\, (x_2, y_2, t),\, s\bigr) = \bigl(x_2 + s(x_1 - x_2),\; y_2 + s(y_1 - y_2),\; t\bigr).
$$
Set $L = \Phi(S)$. As the continuous image of a compact set, $L$ is compact. By construction, for every pair $(x_1, y_1, t), (x_2, y_2, t) \in K$, the entire line segment
$$
\bigl\{\bigl(x_2 + s(x_1 - x_2),\, y_2 + s(y_1 - y_2),\, t\bigr) : s \in [0,1]\bigr\}
$$
is contained in $L$.

**Step 2: Bound the partial derivatives on $L$.**

Since $g \in C^1$, the partial Jacobians $D_x g$ and $D_y g$ (with respect to the $x$- and $y$-variables) are continuous on $\mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^{n'} \times [0,1]$. As operator-valued functions, their operator norms $\|D_x g\|$ and $\|D_y g\|$ are continuous real-valued functions. On the compact set $L$ they attain finite suprema:
$$
M_x = \sup_{(x, y, t) \in L} \|D_x g(x, y, t)\| < \infty, \qquad
M_y = \sup_{(x, y, t) \in L} \|D_y g(x, y, t)\| < \infty.
$$
Set $M = \max(M_x, M_y)$.

**Step 3: Apply the fundamental theorem of calculus.**

Fix $(x_1, y_1, t), (x_2, y_2, t) \in K$. Define $\gamma(s) = (x_2 + s(x_1 - x_2),\; y_2 + s(y_1 - y_2))$ for $s \in [0,1]$. By Step 1, $(\gamma(s), t) \in L$ for all $s \in [0,1]$. Then
$$
g(x_1, y_1, t) - g(x_2, y_2, t) = \int_0^1 \frac{d}{ds}\, g\bigl(\gamma(s), t\bigr)\, ds = \int_0^1 \Bigl[D_x g\bigl(\gamma(s), t\bigr)(x_1 - x_2) + D_y g\bigl(\gamma(s), t\bigr)(y_1 - y_2)\Bigr] ds.
$$
Taking norms and using the triangle inequality,
$$
\|g(x_1, y_1, t) - g(x_2, y_2, t)\| \le \int_0^1 \Bigl[\|D_x g(\gamma(s), t)\|\,\|x_1 - x_2\| + \|D_y g(\gamma(s), t)\|\,\|y_1 - y_2\|\Bigr] ds.
$$
Since $(\gamma(s), t) \in L$, we have $\|D_x g(\gamma(s), t)\| \le M_x \le M$ and $\|D_y g(\gamma(s), t)\| \le M_y \le M$. Therefore
$$
\|g(x_1, y_1, t) - g(x_2, y_2, t)\| \le M\,\|x_1 - x_2\| + M\,\|y_1 - y_2\| = M\bigl(\|x_1 - x_2\| + \|y_1 - y_2\|\bigr).
$$

This holds for every pair in $K$ sharing the same $t$, with the constant $M$ depending only on $K$ (via the compact set $L$) and on $g$. Hence $g$ is locally Lipschitz in the required sense. $\blacksquare$

